commit_report_chapter_batch({'chapters': [{'chapter_id': 1, 'bundle_token': 
'b1-b07734b7', 'content': 'The **Ring of Nestor** is a name associated with an 
engraved gold signet ring showing ornately dressed figures before an altar, a 
scene captioned as an ancient ritual.[1] The available documentation does not, 
however, provide a catalogue record establishing that this ring is an 
archaeological object formally named “Ring of Nestor,” nor does it establish 
the ring’s date, provenance, excavation context, dimensions, or present 
collection.[2] Its reported association is with public presentations of the 
Mycenaean kingdom of Pylos in Messenia, whose palace at Ano Englianos was 
excavated in 1939 and identified in exhibition materials with the Homeric Pylos
of King Nestor.[3] That contextual association does not demonstrate that the 
ring belonged to Nestor or came from the palace.[4]', 'evidence_refs': ['e7', 
'c2', 'e2', 'c4'], 'disclosed_conflict_refs': []}, {'chapter_id': 2, 
'bundle_token': 'b2-49436c1b', 'content': '## Archaeological context\n\nThe 
archaeological setting connected with the name “Nestor” is the Mycenaean palace
at Ano Englianos in Messenia. Carl W. Blegen and Konstantinos Kourouniotis 
excavated the site in 1939; exhibition accounts identify the complex with the 
Homeric Pylos of King Nestor.[1] The palace was built shortly after 1300 BCE 
and functioned as the administrative centre of a kingdom controlling almost the
whole region of modern Messenia.[2] It was destroyed by a major fire around 
1200 BCE, after which the hill of Ano Englianos was abandoned.[3]\n\nThe 
written evidence supports the identification of the place as Pylos but does not
establish a personal connection with the Homeric king. An account of the Linear
B tablets states that they name Pylos, represented as *pu-ro*, while Nestor is 
not named; the tablets mention a *wanax*, a term translated there as “lord”.[4]
Approximately 800 tablets were found in the palace archive at Epano 
Englianos.[5] These facts provide context for the Pylos–Nestor association, but
none of the cited material links the engraved gold ring to the palace, to King 
Nestor, or to a specific excavation inventory.[6]\n\nThe ring should also be 
distinguished from other objects associated with Pylos. The Pylos Combat Agate 
is described as a 3.6-by-2.2-centimetre sealstone depicting three warriors, one
fallen, one being struck by a sword, and a third as victor.[7] The Griffin 
Warrior burial, excavated near Pylos in 2015, contained weapons, armour, 
precious-metal vessels, jewellery, sealstones and other personal objects, but 
the cited descriptions do not identify the engraved signet ring among its 
contents.[8]', 'evidence_refs': ['e2', 'e3', 'e4', 'e18', 'e19', 'c4', 'e20', 
'e21', 'c3'], 'disclosed_conflict_refs': []}, {'chapter_id': 3, 'bundle_token':
'b3-58573de2', 'content': '## Description and interpretation\n\nThe available 
object description identifies an engraved gold ring or signet ring whose image 
shows ornately dressed figures standing before an altar. Its caption interprets
the scene as an ancient ritual.[1] This description is not accompanied by a 
catalogue number, measurements, secure date, excavation record, provenance, or 
named museum collection.[2] The caption also does not call the object “the Ring
of Nestor”; consequently, the identification of the pictured ring with the 
article’s title remains unestablished in the documented material.[3]\n\nThe 
distinction matters because Pylos-related exhibitions present objects from more
than one context. One official exhibition page lists 214 antiquities from the 
Ephorate of Antiquities of Messenia and 11 from the Hellenic National 
Archaeological Museum, while its object captions name items such as a bronze 
crown, a gold-capped agate seal stone, Linear B tablets and a bronze cauldron 
without identifying any of them as the Ring of Nestor.[4] A separate account of
the Getty Villa presentation describes a “Palace of Nestor” section containing 
finds from the palace and adjacent burials within an exhibition of more than 
230 works from Messenia.[5] The same account illustrates the engraved gold 
signet ring, but the supplied description does not resolve whether the ring was
an exhibited object, an associated illustration, or an object from one of those
specific contexts.[6]\n\n## Exhibition chronology\n\nThe official exhibition 
announcement gives two separate dates: the opening was scheduled for 25 May 
2026, and the public exhibition period for 26 May–31 October 2026.[7] The two 
dates describe different stages of the exhibition rather than contradictory 
claims. The exhibition information establishes the public setting in which 
Pylos-related objects were presented, but it does not supply the object-level 
evidence needed to attribute the ring to Nestor, the palace, or a particular 
burial.[8]', 'evidence_refs': ['e7', 'c1', 'c2', 'e5', 'e8', 'c3', 'e6'], 
'disclosed_conflict_refs': ['x1']}]})
