"outputs\\test_v9\\_runs\\82-Ring_of_Nestor\\.report_draft.md", "draft": "# 
Ring of Nestor\n\nThe **Ring of Nestor** is a name associated with an engraved 
gold signet ring showing ornately dressed figures before an altar, a scene 
captioned as an ancient ritual.[2] The available documentation does not, 
however, provide a catalogue record establishing that this ring is an 
archaeological object formally named “Ring of Nestor,” nor does it establish 
the ring’s date, provenance, excavation context, dimensions, or present 
collection.[1][2][4] Its reported association is with public presentations of 
the Mycenaean kingdom of Pylos in Messenia, whose palace at Ano Englianos was 
excavated in 1939 and identified in exhibition materials with the Homeric Pylos
of King Nestor.[1][2] That contextual association does not demonstrate that the
ring belonged to Nestor or came from the palace.[1][2][5]\n\n## Discovery and 
archaeological context\n\n## Archaeological context\n\nThe archaeological 
setting connected with the name “Nestor” is the Mycenaean palace at Ano 
Englianos in Messenia. Carl W. Blegen and Konstantinos Kourouniotis excavated 
the site in 1939; exhibition accounts identify the complex with the Homeric 
Pylos of King Nestor.[1][2] The palace was built shortly after 1300 BCE and 
functioned as the administrative centre of a kingdom controlling almost the 
whole region of modern Messenia.[1] It was destroyed by a major fire around 
1200 BCE, after which the hill of Ano Englianos was abandoned.[1]\n\nThe 
written evidence supports the identification of the place as Pylos but does not
establish a personal connection with the Homeric king. An account of the Linear
B tablets states that they name Pylos, represented as *pu-ro*, while Nestor is 
not named; the tablets mention a *wanax*, a term translated there as “lord”.[5]
Approximately 800 tablets were found in the palace archive at Epano 
Englianos.[5] These facts provide context for the Pylos–Nestor association, but
none of the cited material links the engraved gold ring to the palace, to King 
Nestor, or to a specific excavation inventory.[1][2][5]\n\nThe ring should also
be distinguished from other objects associated with Pylos. The Pylos Combat 
Agate is described as a 3.6-by-2.2-centimetre sealstone depicting three 
warriors, one fallen, one being struck by a sword, and a third as victor.[5] 
The Griffin Warrior burial, excavated near Pylos in 2015, contained weapons, 
armour, precious-metal vessels, jewellery, sealstones and other personal 
objects, but the cited descriptions do not identify the engraved signet ring 
among its contents.[2][5]\n\n## Description, interpretation and public 
display\n\n## Description and interpretation\n\nThe available object 
description identifies an engraved gold ring or signet ring whose image shows 
ornately dressed figures standing before an altar. Its caption interprets the 
