presentations of the Mycenaean kingdom of Pylos in Messenia, whose palace at 
Ano Englianos was excavated in 1939 and identified in exhibition materials with
the Homeric Pylos of King Nestor.[1][2] That contextual association does not 
demonstrate that the ring belonged to Nestor or came from the 
palace.[1][2][5]\n\n## Discovery and archaeological context\n\n## 
Archaeological context\n\nThe archaeological setting connected with the name 
“Nestor” is the Mycenaean palace at Ano Englianos in Messenia. Carl W. Blegen 
and Konstantinos Kourouniotis excavated the site in 1939; exhibition accounts 
identify the complex with the Homeric Pylos of King Nestor.[1][2] The palace 
was built shortly after 1300 BCE and functioned as the administrative centre of
a kingdom controlling almost the whole region of modern Messenia.[1] It was 
destroyed by a major fire around 1200 BCE, after which the hill of Ano 
Englianos was abandoned.[1]\n\nThe written evidence supports the identification
of the place as Pylos but does not establish a personal connection with the 
Homeric king. An account of the Linear B tablets states that they name Pylos, 
represented as *pu-ro*, while Nestor is not named; the tablets mention a 
*wanax*, a term translated there as “lord”.[5] Approximately 800 tablets were 
found in the palace archive at Epano Englianos.[5] These facts provide context 
for the Pylos–Nestor association, but none of the cited material links the 
engraved gold ring to the palace, to King Nestor, or to a specific excavation 
inventory.[1][2][5]\n\nThe ring should also be distinguished from other objects
associated with Pylos. The Pylos Combat Agate is described as a 
3.6-by-2.2-centimetre sealstone depicting three warriors, one fallen, one being
struck by a sword, and a third as victor.[5] The Griffin Warrior burial, 
excavated near Pylos in 2015, contained weapons, armour, precious-metal 
vessels, jewellery, sealstones and other personal objects, but the cited 
descriptions do not identify the engraved signet ring among its 
contents.[2][5]\n\n## Description, interpretation and public display\n\n## 
Description, interpretation and public display\n\nThe available object 
description identifies an engraved gold ring or signet ring whose image shows 
ornately dressed figures standing before an altar. Its caption interprets the 
scene as an ancient ritual.[2] This description is not accompanied by a 
catalogue number, measurements, secure date, excavation record, provenance, or 
named museum collection.[2][1][4] The caption also does not call the object 
“the Ring of Nestor”; consequently, the identification of the pictured ring 
with the article’s title remains unestablished in the documented 
material.[1][2][4]\n\nThe distinction matters because Pylos-related exhibitions
present objects from more than one context. One official exhibition page lists 
214 antiquities from the Ephorate of Antiquities of Messenia and 11 from the 
Hellenic National Archaeological Museum, while its object captions name items 
such as a bronze crown, a gold-capped agate seal stone, Linear B tablets and a 
bronze cauldron without identifying any of them as the Ring of Nestor.[1] A 
separate account of the Getty Villa presentation describes a “Palace of Nestor”
section containing finds from the palace and adjacent burials within an 
exhibition of more than 230 works from Messenia.[2] The same account 
illustrates the engraved gold signet ring, but the supplied description does 
not resolve whether the ring was an exhibited object, an associated 
illustration, or an object from one of those specific contexts.[2][1]\n\nThe 
official announcement distinguishes the exhibition opening, scheduled for 25 
May 2026, from the public exhibition period, scheduled for 26 May–31 October 
2026.[1] These are two different dates for two different stages of the same 
exhibition, not contradictory accounts.[1] The exhibition information 
establishes the public setting in which Pylos-related objects were presented, 
but it does not supply the object-level evidence needed to attribute the ring 
to Nestor, the palace, or a particular burial.[1][2][4] The ring’s formal name,
provenance, date, excavation context, dimensions and present collection 
therefore remain unidentified in the supplied documentation.[1][2][4]\n\n## 
References\n\n[1] Pylos of Nestor A Mycenaean Kingdom Revealed 
https://www.namuseum.gr/en/temporary_exhibition/pylos-of-nestor-a-mycenaean-kin
gdom-revealed\n\n[2] Bronze Age artifacts go on display in North America for 
