commit_report_chapter_batch({'chapters': [{'chapter_id': 1, 'bundle_token': 
'b1-b07734b7', 'content': 'The **Ring of Nestor** is a name associated with an 
engraved gold signet ring showing ornately dressed figures before an altar. A 
University of Cincinnati article captions the scene as an ancient ritual.[2] 
The article does not identify the object by the name “Ring of Nestor,” and the 
cited exhibition materials do not provide a catalogue record establishing its 
date, provenance, excavation context, dimensions, or present 
collection.[1][2][4] The name links the ring in public presentation to the 
Mycenaean kingdom of Pylos in Messenia, whose palace at Ano Englianos was 
excavated in 1939 and identified in exhibition materials with the Homeric Pylos
of King Nestor.[1][2] That contextual association does not establish that the 
ring belonged to Nestor or came from the palace.[1][2][5]', 'evidence_refs': 
['e7', 'c2', 'e2', 'c4'], 'disclosed_conflict_refs': []}, {'chapter_id': 2, 
'bundle_token': 'b2-49436c1b', 'content': 'Carl W. Blegen and Konstantinos 
Kourouniotis excavated the Mycenaean palace at Ano Englianos in Messenia in 
1939; exhibition materials identify the complex with the Homeric Pylos of King 
Nestor.[1][2] The palace was built shortly after 1300 BCE and functioned as the
administrative centre of a kingdom controlling almost the whole region of 
modern Messenia.[1] A major fire destroyed it around 1200 BCE, after which the 
hill of Ano Englianos was abandoned.[1]\n\nThe Linear B tablets name Pylos, 
represented as *pu-ro*, but do not name Nestor; they mention a *wanax*, 
translated as “lord”.[5] Approximately 800 tablets were found in the palace 
archive at Epano Englianos.[5] This evidence supports the Pylos context but 
does not link the engraved gold ring to the palace, King Nestor, or a specific 
excavation inventory.[1][2][5]\n\nThe ring is distinct from the Pylos Combat 
Agate, a 3.6-by-2.2-centimetre sealstone depicting three warriors.[5] The 
Griffin Warrior burial, excavated near Pylos in 2015, contained jewellery and 
sealstones, but the cited descriptions do not identify the engraved ring among 
its contents.[2][5]', 'evidence_refs': ['e2', 'e3', 'e4', 'e18', 'e19', 'c4', 
'e20', 'e21', 'c3'], 'disclosed_conflict_refs': []}, {'chapter_id': 3, 
'bundle_token': 'b3-58573de2', 'content': 'The University of Cincinnati 
article’s image caption describes an engraved gold ring showing ornately 
dressed figures standing before an altar and labels the scene an ancient 
ritual.[2] The caption does not identify the object as “the Ring of Nestor.” It
gives no catalogue number, measurements, secure date, excavation record, 
provenance, or named museum collection.[2][1][4] The image therefore documents 
a visual description and a captioned interpretation, but does not establish the
object’s formal name or archaeological attribution.[1][2][4]\n\nThe same 
University of Cincinnati article describes the Getty Villa exhibition “The 
Kingdom of Pylos: Warrior-Princes of Mycenaean Greece” and its “Palace of 
Nestor” section of finds from the palace and adjacent burials.[2] It reports 
more than 230 works from Messenia and illustrates the engraved gold ring, but 
does not establish whether the pictured ring came from the palace, an adjacent 
burial, or another context.[2] The official National Archaeological Museum 
announcement separately lists an exhibition of 214 antiquities from the 
Ephorate of Antiquities of Messenia and 11 from the Hellenic National 
Archaeological Museum; its listed object captions do not identify any object as
the Ring of Nestor.[1]\n\nThe National Archaeological Museum announcement gives
25 May 2026 as the exhibition opening and 26 May–31 October 2026 as the public 
exhibition period.[1] These dates refer to different stages of the same 
exhibition rather than contradictory accounts.[1] The ring’s formal name, 
provenance, date, excavation context, dimensions, and present collection remain
unidentified in the cited object descriptions.[1][2][4]', 'evidence_refs': 
['e7', 'c1', 'c2', 'e8', 'e5', 'e6'], 'disclosed_conflict_refs': ['x1']}]})
