"outputs\\test_v9\\_runs\\82-Ring_of_Nestor\\.report_draft.md", "draft": "# 
Ring of Nestor\n\nThe **Ring of Nestor** is a name associated with an engraved 
gold signet ring showing ornately dressed figures before an altar. A University
of Cincinnati article’s image caption interprets the scene as an ancient 
ritual.[2] The caption does not identify the object by the name “Ring of 
Nestor.” The National Archaeological Museum announcement and the University of 
Cincinnati article do not provide a catalogue record establishing its date, 
provenance, excavation context, dimensions, or present collection.[1][2] The 
name connects the ring in public presentation with the Mycenaean kingdom of 
Pylos in Messenia, whose palace at Ano Englianos was excavated in 1939 and 
identified in exhibition materials with the Homeric Pylos of King Nestor.[1][2]
That contextual connection does not establish that the ring belonged to Nestor 
or came from the palace.[1][2][5]\n\n## Discovery and archaeological 
context\n\nCarl W. Blegen and Konstantinos Kourouniotis excavated the Mycenaean
palace at Ano Englianos in Messenia in 1939; the National Archaeological Museum
and University of Cincinnati accounts identify the complex with the Homeric 
Pylos of King Nestor.[1][2] The palace was built shortly after 1300 BCE and 
functioned as the administrative centre of a kingdom controlling almost the 
whole region of modern Messenia.[1] A major fire destroyed it around 1200 BCE, 
after which the hill of Ano Englianos was abandoned.[1]\n\nThe Linear B tablets
name Pylos, represented as *pu-ro*, but do not name Nestor; they mention a 
*wanax*, translated as “lord”.[5] Approximately 800 tablets were found in the 
palace archive at Epano Englianos.[5] This evidence supports the Pylos context 
but does not link the engraved gold ring to the palace, King Nestor, or a 
specific excavation inventory.[1][2][5]\n\nThe ring is distinct from the Pylos 
Combat Agate, a 3.6-by-2.2-centimetre sealstone depicting three warriors.[5] 
The Griffin Warrior burial, excavated near Pylos in 2015, contained jewellery 
and sealstones, but the University of Cincinnati article and The Past account 
do not identify the engraved ring among its contents.[2][5] These objects 
provide context for Pylos-related archaeology, not evidence of the ring’s 
provenance.[2][5]\n\n## Description, interpretation and public display\n\nThe 
University of Cincinnati article’s image caption describes an engraved gold 
ring showing ornately dressed figures standing before an altar and labels the 
scene an ancient ritual.[2] The caption does not identify the object as “the 
Ring of Nestor.” It gives no catalogue number, measurements, secure date, 
excavation record, provenance, or named museum collection.[2][1] Thus, the 
caption documents a visual description and a captioned interpretation, but does
not establish the object’s formal name or archaeological 
attribution.[1][2]\n\nThe University of Cincinnati article describes the Getty 
Villa exhibition “The Kingdom of Pylos: Warrior-Princes of Mycenaean Greece” 
and its “Palace of Nestor” section of finds from the palace and adjacent 
burials.[2] It reports more than 230 works from Messenia and illustrates the 
engraved gold ring, but does not establish whether the pictured ring came from 
the palace, an adjacent burial, or another context.[2] The National 
Archaeological Museum announcement lists Pylos-related antiquities and their 
captions, but none identifies an object as the Ring of Nestor.[1]\n\nThe 
National Archaeological Museum announcement gives 25 May 2026 as the exhibition
opening and 26 May–31 October 2026 as the public exhibition period.[1] It 
therefore distinguishes two dates for different stages of the same exhibition, 
rather than presenting contradictory dates.[1] The ring’s formal name, 
provenance, date, excavation context, dimensions, and present collection remain
unidentified in the cited object descriptions.[1][2]\n\n## References\n\n[1] 
Pylos of Nestor A Mycenaean Kingdom Revealed 
https://www.namuseum.gr/en/temporary_exhibition/pylos-of-nestor-a-mycenaean-kin
gdom-revealed\n\n[2] Bronze Age artifacts go on display in North America for 
first time - University of Cincinnati: Founder of Co-op, Leader in Co-op | 
University of Cincinnati 
https://www.uc.edu/news/articles/2025/05/bronze-age-artifacts-in-north-america-
first-time.html\n\n[5] Pylos: From princes to a palace in Messenia – The Past 
https://the-past.com/feature/pylos-from-princes-to-a-palace-in-messenia\n", 
"issues": [{"code": "invalid_conflict_disclosure_binding", "severity": "hard", 
