──────────────────────────────── Final Report ─────────────────────────────────
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                               Ring of Nestor                                │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

The Ring of Nestor is a name associated with an engraved gold signet ring      
showing ornately dressed figures before an altar. A University of Cincinnati   
article’s image caption interprets the scene as an ancient ritual.[2] The      
caption does not identify the object by the name “Ring of Nestor.” The National
Archaeological Museum announcement and the University of Cincinnati article do 
not provide a catalogue record establishing its date, provenance, excavation   
context, dimensions, or present collection.[1][2] The name connects the ring in
public presentation with the Mycenaean kingdom of Pylos in Messenia, whose     
palace at Ano Englianos was excavated in 1939 and identified in exhibition     
materials with the Homeric Pylos of King Nestor.[1][2] That contextual         
connection does not establish that the ring belonged to Nestor or came from the
palace.[1][2][5]                                                               


                     Discovery and archaeological context                      

Carl W. Blegen and Konstantinos Kourouniotis excavated the Mycenaean palace at 
Ano Englianos in Messenia in 1939; the National Archaeological Museum and      
University of Cincinnati accounts identify the complex with the Homeric Pylos  
of King Nestor.[1][2] The palace was built shortly after 1300 BCE and          
functioned as the administrative centre of a kingdom controlling almost the    
whole region of modern Messenia.[1] A major fire destroyed it around 1200 BCE, 
after which the hill of Ano Englianos was abandoned.[1]                        

The Linear B tablets name Pylos, represented as pu-ro, but do not name Nestor; 
they mention a wanax, translated as “lord”.[5] Approximately 800 tablets were  
found in the palace archive at Epano Englianos.[5] This evidence supports the  
Pylos context but does not link the engraved gold ring to the palace, King     
Nestor, or a specific excavation inventory.[1][2][5] The Pylos Combat Agate is 
a separate 3.6-by-2.2-centimetre sealstone depicting three warriors, and the   
Griffin Warrior burial is a separate burial containing jewellery and           
sealstones; neither is identified as the engraved ring.[2][5]                  


                Description, interpretation and public display                 

The University of Cincinnati article’s image caption describes an engraved gold
ring showing ornately dressed figures standing before an altar and labels the  
scene an ancient ritual.[2] The caption does not identify the object as “the   
Ring of Nestor.” It gives no catalogue number, measurements, secure date,      
excavation record, provenance, or named museum collection.[2][1] Thus, the     
caption supports a visual description and a captioned interpretation, but not  
the object’s formal name or archaeological attribution.[1][2]                  

The University of Cincinnati article describes the Getty Villa exhibition “The 
Kingdom of Pylos: Warrior-Princes of Mycenaean Greece” and its “Palace of      
Nestor” section of finds from the palace and adjacent burials.[2] The article  
illustrates the engraved gold ring, but does not establish whether the pictured
ring came from the palace, an adjacent burial, or another context.[2] The      
National Archaeological Museum announcement lists Pylos-related antiquities and
their captions, but none identifies an object as the Ring of Nestor.[1]        

The National Archaeological Museum announcement gives 25 May 2026 as the       
exhibition opening and 26 May–31 October 2026 as the public exhibition         
period.[1] These dates refer to different stages of the same exhibition: 25 May
2026 was the opening, while 26 May–31 October 2026 was the stated public       
period.[1] This distinction is not a contradiction. The ring’s formal name,    
provenance, date, excavation context, dimensions, and present collection remain
unidentified in the cited object descriptions.[1][2]                           


                                  References                                   

[1] Pylos of Nestor A Mycenaean Kingdom Revealed                               
https://www.namuseum.gr/en/temporary_exhibition/pylos-of-nestor-a-mycenaean-kin
gdom-revealed                                                                  

[2] Bronze Age artifacts go on display in North America for first time -       
University of Cincinnati: Founder of Co-op, Leader in Co-op | University of    
Cincinnati                                                                     
https://www.uc.edu/news/articles/2025/05/bronze-age-artifacts-in-north-america-
first-time.html                                                                

[5] Pylos: From princes to a palace in Messenia – The Past                     
https://the-past.com/feature/pylos-from-princes-to-a-palace-in-messenia        

